test/68k-console -- instruments for console (SCF) behaviourWhat is here, and why it is not in test/68k-conformance:CONF68K is a suite that must run unattended, from a procedure file, on asystem nobody is sitting at. Anything that READS standard input can neverjoin it -- runall would stop dead waiting for a human. So console-inputinstruments live here instead, and are driven by the emulator's own Swiftharness (test/Sources/OS9Tests), which can feed them a line of known length.rdlnecho    Reads ONE line from standard input with a 1024-byte count, then writes    back exactly the bytes I$ReadLn returned.rdecho    The same, but one raw I$Read instead. It separates a console read that    stops at PD_EOR from one that runs on past it, which shows only when a    SECOND record is already buffered behind the first -- the harness feeds    stdin from a pipe, so it always is.    It exists because nothing on a v2.4 system disk asks for more than 512    bytes on a console read -- measured by `-d 2` trace: the shell asks $200,    BASIC09 $1FF, and bash, mshell, kermit, vi and umacs all $200 or less --    so no shipped program can tell a system that caps a console I$ReadLn at    SCF's line-editing buffer from one that honours whatever the caller asks.    What you see is the line itself, not a number the module reports about    itself, so there is nothing to take on trust: feed it a line longer than    the cap and count what comes back.rdtoeof    Copies standard input to standard output a line at a time until a read    fails, then writes END. It is for the HOST side of standard input: on    Windows a redirected file used to give nothing and a pipe never ended,    so a filter hung. tools/verify-winvm.sh runs it from a file, a pipe and    NUL, since only a Windows host can reach that code; END is the proof    that the end arrived.Both the source and the built module are committed, exactly as CONF68K doesit, so the instrument runs on a system with no SDK installed.REBUILDINGNeeds this system'"'"'s own r68 and l68, and the emulator to reach thisdirectory as a device. From the repo root:    OS9DISK=<your system disk> OS9H6="$PWD/test/68k-console" \      ./os9exec -r shellthen, inside:    chd /h6/SRC    r68 rdlnecho.a -o=/h6/SCRATCH/rdlnecho.r    l68 /h6/SCRATCH/rdlnecho.r -o=/h6/CMDS/rdlnecho    r68 rdecho.a -o=/h6/SCRATCH/rdecho.r    l68 /h6/SCRATCH/rdecho.r -o=/h6/CMDS/rdecho    r68 rdtoeof.a -o=/h6/SCRATCH/rdtoeof.r    l68 /h6/SCRATCH/rdtoeof.r -o=/h6/CMDS/rdtoeofGive l68'"'"'s -o= an ABSOLUTE path. It resolves a relative one against yourEXECUTION directory, not the data directory it read the .r from, so a bare-o=rdlnecho writes the module into your system command directory instead.The same trap is documented at length in test/68k-conformance/readme.Line endings here are CR, as OS-9 requires. A source with LF endings reads asone enormous line to the assembler.